 that he was  terminated rather abruptly and that it  was connected to allegations of  misconduct but the story with his player  and you know for anyone who read the  investigation the opening anecdote  regarding  um an incident that happened one-on-one  with a player during a film room  um viewing  none of that was known prior to this  report so all of that was rather  staggering and I think more than  anything the depth of knowledge of all  of this was alarming for everyone one  yeah Molly the Fallout has been huge  head start rolling as soon as the report  came out but that's not going to solve  the problem is it  I wish it did but it's not it needs more  than that there was tremendous reporting  that Meg linahan and Molly Hensley  Clancy did last year that really got the  AIDS investigation started back then you  saw five of the ten coaches were out of  the league by the end of the year but  then you have a year later the Yates  report comes out and there's more we  don't even know so getting rid of a few  people in the report isn't going to  solve the problem at its core which is a  league in which misconduct has been  systemic across coaches players leagues  it it needs a complete overhaul  how can it be tackled though Meredith  where to start this is a web of abuse  condemnation complicity and fear where  do you start to overhaul that and  rebuild the structure basically from  scratch  I think the biggest issue that was laid  out pretty well in an E60 report that  aired the night after Sally yates's  report was released  um was you know the fundamental issues  at the core of this that allowed such  misconduct proliferate  um and that's the fact that there's  insecurity within uh women's  professional soccer at large and there  has been since its founding before the  national women's soccer league  um came to be in 2013 there were two  previous leagues both of which folded  after three years and so by the time you  get to the nwsl players who are there  are just feeling grateful to have a  position to play professional soccer and  so they tolerated behavior and  um  facilities and compensation that was  just substandard as a result no one  wanted to be the Whistleblower who  brought the entire league down and so  they put up with things that they  shouldn't have and so I think part of  the project that is rebuilding the nwsl  that's already underway for what it's  worth since these initial allegations  came to light late last year  um is to is to build a stronger  structurally sound League that doesn't  have these loopholes that allows players  to feel confident in speaking their  truth and and demanding what they're  worth Molly you have looked into the  response of the nwsl sponsors to the  report and they seem to be struggling to  say the least to find an appropriate way  to react don't they  struggling is to put it a little lightly  but to be fair they were probably pretty  blindsided just as a lot of us were that  hadn't seen the report and didn't fully  know the extent but my colleague Jason  Noddy and I spent pretty much 48 hours  straight after the report came out  reaching out to sponsors across the  league  um and specific teams as well  specifically teams like the red stars  the Portland Thorns racing Louisville  teams that were really named in the  report so we reached out to 19 we heard  back from ten since then a couple more  have released statements but it's been a  lot of corporate jargon a lot of deeply  Disturbed which I'm sure they are as we  all are  but I think what a lot of these Brands  and sponsors either don't want to  realize or just haven't realized is the  power a lot of the power lies within  them too it's been a lot of we can't we  need to see what the nwsl does we're  hoping for change so we all are but if  these Brands want to continue sponsoring  the nwsl and I hope they do because I  don't want the league to fold nobody  wants the league to fold and you need  sponsors they need to use their power as  sponsors and their spending power to try  and force the nwsl end to change and for  what it's worth I really do believe US  soccer and the nwsl are committed to  meaningful change and have already  started making those steps but one  sponsored reaction that really did stand  out to me was Ally Financial  they're a nationwide sponsor they're the  lead kit sponsor or sleeve sponsor so  their CMO Andrea Brimmer is calling for  Meaningful change to find the truth no  matter how painful and I'd like to see  more Brands react like that  how have the teams reacted because this  investigation was focused on three teams  in particular Meredith did one of these  teams stick out with a particularly a  good response in your view  honestly no I wouldn't say that any one  team's response has been  um particularly impressive the Portland  Thorns did part ways with two Executives  who were known for a while now to have  been aware of the abuse and have  essentially turned a blind eye and given  a vote of confidence to Paul Reilly as  he went from the Portland Thorns to the  North Carolina courage another team in  the league  um  but you know the Portland Thorns owner  Merritt Paulson was also aware of these  allegations also voiced confidence and  support for  Paul Riley  as he moved on to another team within  the league knowing what he had done  within their own franchise  um and you know as long as he is the  person who has the most power in that  franchise I don't think that's good  enough personally and I think a lot of  fans and a lot of players have voiced  the same opinion Becky sauerbrun the  player you just uh played a sound bite  from voice the same thing during her  recent um her recent interviews and I  think that's true across the board right  I think in Louisville with Christy Holly  and with Rory Dames in Chicago with the  red stars there were people in positions  of power who knew and are still in  positions of power within those  franchises and none o